[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fuzz_diff.py` and `fuzz_heap.py`, run the same comparison over randomly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m generated command streams; ~3200 random cases were run while writing this and all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m were byte-identical.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. The path array can be handed a freed node's chunk, and the C program dies
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `find_shortest_path` ends with `malloc(sizeof(node_t*) * count)`. For a path of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 30 or 31 nodes that request (240 or 248 bytes) falls in the same glibc size class
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m as `node_t` (chunk size 256). If a node has been deleted to a reference count of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0 beforehand, its chunk is sitting in the tcache and is handed to that `malloc`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so `result[i] = ...` overwrites the node's fields with node addresses:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `city_name` becomes the first eight pointers,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `ref_count` (offset 64) becomes the low half of `path[8]`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `edges[0..=10]` become pointers and pointer halves,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `edge_count` (offset 232) becomes the low half of `path[29]`, i.e. a number in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the millions.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The next `3` (show all cities) then prints that wreckage and keeps walking
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `edges[i]`. At `i == 11` the read lands past the 248 usable bytes of the chunk, on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the next chunk's size field (`0x101`), and dereferencing that address kills the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m process: `SIGSEGV`, exit status 139, with everything still in the block-buffered
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout buffer discarded (16384 of 17554 bytes reach the pipe in the reference
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m case).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Before the fix the Rust program ran to completion and exited 0. Two things were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m missing:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `Heap` modelled a freed chunk's contents only as far as the eight bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `free()` writes; the stores into the path array were not modelled at all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`alloc_scratch` was immediately followed by `free_scratch` and neither touched
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the aliased node).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `free(path)` happens in `main` *after* the path is printed. The model did it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   inside `find_shortest_path`, so the order of "array contents overwrite the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   node" and "free() writes its next pointer over the first eight bytes" was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   wrong. That ordering is observable: the name printed later is the safe-linked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   pointer, not the first array entry.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fixed in `src/dag_lib.rs`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `Heap::corrupt` keeps a byte image of any chunk that was reused for something
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   that is not a `node_t`, and `name`, `ref_count`, `edge_count`, `read_edge` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `push_edge` read and write through it at the ABI offsets of `node_t`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `Heap::write_scratch` applies the stores the path array makes;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `Heap::free_scratch` leaves the safe-linked next pointer behind, like
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `Heap::free`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `read_edge` returns `EdgeRead::Wild` for an offset past the usable chunk (or an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   address that is not a node), and every place the C code dereferences such a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   pointer calls `segfault()`, which performs the same read of an unmapped
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   address. In `print_node` the literal `"    -> "` is appended to the stdout
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   buffer first, because that is what glibc's `printf` does before it touches the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `%s` argument.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `find_shortest_path` now returns a `PathArray` and `main` calls `free_path`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   where `main.c` calls `free(path)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified for path lengths 28 to 33 (only 30 and 31 crash) and with the crash
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pushed to seven different offsets inside the 4096-byte stdout block, with ASLR
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m both enabled and disabled: stdout, stderr and exit status are identical
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`tests/heap.rs`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. Reachable double free
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Found while probing the same area, and already handled correctly by the existing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `glibc_abort` emulation, but worth recording because it looks unreachable and is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. `1 A` then `7 A` frees A's chunk. `city_name` now holds the safe-linked next
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    pointer, which for the reference build is the two bytes `08 04`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. `6 <0x08><0x04>` — those bytes typed as a city name — makes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `get_node_by_name` match the freed node, and `shallow_copy` pushes its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    reference count from 0 back to 1.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. On exit, `free_graph` decrements it to 0 and calls `free()` on a chunk that is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    already in the tcache. glibc prints `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    and aborts: exit status 134, buffered stdout lost.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both programs agree, including the message and the lost stdout
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`tests/refcount.rs::a_shallow_copy_of_a_freed_node_makes_the_exit_double_free`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Things that look like mismatches but are properties of the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### The C build configuration changes the behaviour of the undefined parts
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Built as the task describes (`cmake ..`, i.e. no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, so no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m optimisation) the input `1 / A / 7 / A / 8` runs to completion and exits 0. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m same source configured with `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` aborts with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `free(): double free detected in tcache 2`, because `delete_node`'s
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `node->ref_count--` and the following comparison are reordered around the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `free()` in a way the unoptimised build does not do. The Rust program matches the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unoptimised build, which is the one the quoted build commands produce. Anything
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that reads a freed node is undefined behaviour in C and cannot be matched by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *any* implementation across compiler settings.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Reads of freed memory are only reproducible with ASLR disabled
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Where the C program prints bytes out of a freed chunk (`City: <name>` after a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m delete, the numbers in the wrecked node above) it is printing pieces of heap
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m addresses. With ASLR on, the C program produces different output on every run;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with ASLR off it is stable, and `HEAP_NODE_BASE` in `src/dag_lib.rs` is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m address the reference build uses in that configuration. The suite therefore runs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m those cases through `setarch -R`. If a machine cannot disable ASLR, the harness
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m runs the C program twice and requires the Rust program to match the part the C
