[1mWhat was wrong.[22m One real defect, in the heap emulation the translation uses to reproduce the C program's use-after-free behaviour. [38;5;10mfind_shortest_path[0m ends with [38;5;10mmalloc(count * sizeof(node_t*))[0m; for a path of exactly 30 or 31 nodes that request lands in the same glibc size class as [38;5;10mnode_t[0m, so if a node had been deleted to reference count 0 its recycled chunk becomes the path array. The stores into the array overwrite the node's fields with node addresses — [38;5;10medge_count[0m becomes the low half of a pointer, i.e. millions — and the next "show all cities" prints the wreckage, then reads past the chunk onto the next chunk's size field and dies: SIGSEGV, 16384 of 17554 stdout bytes flushed, the rest lost in the buffer. The Rust program ran to completion and exited 0.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two things were missing: the model never applied the array's stores to the aliased chunk, and it freed the array inside [38;5;10mfind_shortest_path[0m when [38;5;10mmain.c[0m frees it [3mafter[23m printing — an ordering that is observable, because the name printed later is the safe-linked pointer [38;5;10mfree()[0m leaves behind, not the first array entry. Fixed by giving [38;5;10mHeap[0m a byte-level image of any chunk reused as something other than a [38;5;10mnode_t[0m, reading [38;5;10mname[0m/[38;5;10mref_count[0m/[38;5;10medge_count[0m/[38;5;10medges[0m through it at the ABI offsets, returning a "wild" edge for offsets past the chunk, dying the same way the C does (including the partial [38;5;10m"    -> "[0m glibc's [38;5;10mprintf[0m puts in the buffer before it touches the [38;5;10m%s[0m), and moving [38;5;10mfree(path)[0m into [38;5;10mmain[0m. Verified for path lengths 28–33 and with the crash pushed to seven different offsets inside the 4096-byte block, with ASLR on and off.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mTwo findings worth your attention, recorded in [38;5;10mtranslation/ERRORS.md[0m:[22m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- The C build configuration changes the observable behaviour of the undefined parts. [38;5;10m1/A/7/A/8[0m exits 0 when built as the task specifies ([38;5;10mcmake ..[0m, unoptimised) but aborts with [38;5;10mfree(): double free detected in tcache 2[0m when configured [38;5;10mRelease[0m, because [38;5;10mref_count--[0m gets reordered around the [38;5;10mfree()[0m. The Rust matches the unoptimised build.[0m[0m
- Reads of freed memory print pieces of heap addresses, so the C program is only self-reproducible with ASLR disabled. Those tests run both binaries under [38;5;10msetarch -R[0m; if a machine cannot disable ASLR, the harness runs the C twice and requires the Rust to match the part the C reproduces, still comparing stderr and exit status exactly.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One difference is documented but not fixed: if the reader of stdout closes early, the C dies from SIGPIPE while the Rust exits 0, because the Rust runtime ignores SIGPIPE and restoring the default needs either a [38;5;10mlibc[0m dependency or arch-specific inline assembly. It is unreachable when the streams are captured to completion, which is how the programs are compared. [38;5;10mc_src/[0m is unmodified apart from the [38;5;10mbuild/[0m directory the quoted commands create.[0m[0m
